WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.500
- You've been out on the front lines

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.020
at protests recently.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.760
Has that experience given you hope?

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.710
Or has it kind of deterred you?

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:07.543
I feel like

00:00:07.543 --> 00:00:09.350
I know several people that have gone

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:10.970
and there've been
different kinds of protests

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.730
and different happenings in each one.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.580
So what is your experience been like?

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.642
- It makes me feel hopeful personally

00:00:17.642 --> 00:00:21.860
because the protests have
not stopped since May.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.260
And we're now in June

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.940
and I don't see them
slowing down or stopping.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.380
- Is there any particular

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.420
moment that has really stood out for you?

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.120
Like since May and now
we're moving into June?

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.140
- Um, no particular moments.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:35.973
I mean, it's just

00:00:35.973 --> 00:00:37.220
really depressing to wake up,

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:38.240
open Twitter and go

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.073
Oh, okay.

00:00:39.073 --> 00:00:40.490
- Yeah
- Somebody else is dead

00:00:41.377 --> 00:00:42.210
- Yeah.
- Yeah

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.043
And I don't know

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:44.110
if things will go back to normal.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.310
I mean, at least not for black people.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:49.290
We talk about it ad nauseum.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.840
I talk about it in my stand ups.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:51.760
So like, I don't know

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.330
if there is like a going back.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.530
Like if you're not
radicalized for forever,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.040
like, you're part of the problem.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.410
And I encourage a lot of white people

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:00.700
to read white fragility.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.840
It is a wonderful book

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.110
that truly breaks down

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.120
why white people don't
wanna talk about race.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.513
And why their discomfort seems to,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.140
you know, take precedent over learning.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:12.996
- And why, like,

00:01:12.996 --> 00:01:14.830
i think a lot of people

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:15.730
don't even notice it.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:16.580
They don't see it.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:17.413
You know what I'm saying?

00:01:17.413 --> 00:01:18.246
- Oh yeah
- It's like f you're in it,

00:01:18.246 --> 00:01:19.930
you don't even see it a lot sometimes.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:20.763
And its

00:01:20.763 --> 00:01:21.630
- Oh, yeah absolutely
- Not until you listen

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:22.640
and have conversations

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.260
to where you see the difference.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:25.660
And you hear the difference

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.220
that you actually go, hmm okay?

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.053
Like, you know

00:01:29.053 --> 00:01:29.886
I think that's a big problem

00:01:29.886 --> 00:01:30.870
about it too, is the ignorance.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.140
I think educating oneself

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.300
is probably the most important thing

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.510
you can do right now.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:36.510
Other than listening

